 you're doing skills overlap because  it's one thing to just say skills only  approach but actually what we've seen so  far and the serious way to do a skills  only approach which is what we're trying  to do at Kaiser in the place as I  mentioned and where they and that  they've demonstrated to work in Canada  and to the University of Toronto with  Shelley macmaine's more recent study is  that you do include their whole package  of skills training you do include the  therapists have a team meeting so the  consultation team is still included you  still include coaching in between  sessions by telephone though actually in  Shelley macmaine study in Canada there  was very little of that that actually  went on even though they were ready to  handle it and still the findings were  good so that actually was mostly skills  and finally it includes the therapists  knowing the suicide crisis protocol to  assess identify and manage a high  suicide risk if you put that package  together what are you leaving out you're  leaving out individual therapy conducted  according to the DBT manual you might  have a different individual therapy but  so mainly the thing you're leaving out  is the really DBT based individual  therapy okay we're getting some great  questions I don't know if I'll have time  to get to all of them and if so then  we'll definitely bring them up to the  panel what would you do differently for  someone who's already in DBT for  symptoms of PTSD is there something you  would do differently so somebody enters  DBT and they are and they have a lot of  dysregulated behaviors a lot of pain a  lot of sufferings going on and they're  to some degree out of control in their  life and they have PTSD and that's part  of the picture they're having flashbacks  they're having nightmares they're having  intrusive thoughts they get caught in  these  memory traps that they get into they get  triggered into things so this is not an  unusual group within the borderline  personality disorder population right so  what do you do if you know somebody has  PTSD from the beginning well I think  first of all you orient the patient that  you're going to treat the whole thing  that that is central to the treatment is  treating PTSD because some people enter  DBT and say you're telling me I just  have to learn skills and I know we're  not telling you that we're telling you  you have to learn skills on the way to  becoming more resilient on the way to  treating the things that have caused  your suffering in your life and if  that's PTSD for you that's PTSD so the  first important thing is to make it  clear this is within the treatment  package it's a stepwise approach in  treatment it isn't left out a lot of  people bail out of DBT in stage one  because they actually want to talk about  their trauma and the other thing is that  when I work with people that have trauma  based problems when I'm doing the work  in stage one of trying to help them get  their behavior under control over and  over again when we look at scripts of  behavior behavioral shayne analyses you  find here and there and all over the  script there's evidence of the PTSD you  stop right there  you acknowledge it you validate it you  label it you say we're going to treat  this you help with those symptoms right  now and but then you try to get the  person to the point where you can  actually go in more depth and get the  whole story more in a more comprehensive  way within the therapy which is the  treatment of the PTSD along the lines of  the current protocol we've got by  Melanie Harned from Seattle  Oh Melanie is doing the callin next  Sunday night let me just you know she's  fabulous at this and it's really it's a  hype she's hybrid she's created the  synthesis between DBT and prolonged  exposure treatment for trauma and she's  really put them together in a brilliant  way and now she's gathering data for  that so you should tune into that and  that's the NEA Collins series that you  can find the details out for on the NEA  website which you will also find in the  beginning pages of the booklet um last  question for right now before we take a  break for lunch is that we have a  question asking about the effectiveness  of DBT for males suffering from BPD are  you aware of efficacy studies  specifically in males  well what's changed from those first  studies is that males have been included  in most programs and in studies and so  what's what has been noticed in those  studies that have males and females is  that actually the males are doing as  well as the females there's also been  more recent data just epidemiologically  that makes it clearer that whereas we  used to think that about 80% of people  with borderline personality disorder are  females that actually that's not true  that it's closer to 5050 than it looked  if you include things that usually are  sort of male specialties of disorders of  over control certain impulsive behaviors  antisocial behaviors but yes it looks  like the data is just as good for males  as it is for females all right well  thank you very much dr. Scott you're  welcome thank you
 

